@c bib -> itexi intro
@itemize

@item
G. Assayaag and D. Timis.
@strong{A Toolbox for music notation}.
In @emph{Proceedings of the 1986 International Computer Music Conference},
  1986.





@item
M. Balaban.
@strong{A Music Workstation Based on Multiple Hierarchical Views of Music}.
San Francisco, In @emph{Proceedings of the 1988 International Computer Music
  Conference}, 1988.





@item
Alan Belkin.
@strong{Macintosh Notation Software: Present and Future}.
@emph{Computer Music Journal}, 18(1), 1994.
Some music notation systems are analysed for ease of use, MIDI handling. The
  article ends with a plea for a standard notation format. HWN.




@item
Herbert Bielawa.
@strong{Review of Sibelius 7}.
@emph{Computer Music Journal}, 1993?.
A raving review/tutorial of Sibelius 7 for Acorn. (And did they seriously
  program a RISC chip in ... assembler ?!) HWN.




@item
Dorothea Blostein and Lippold Haken.
@strong{Justification of Printed Music}.
@emph{Communications of the ACM}, J34(3):88-99, March 1991.
This paper provides an overview of the algorithm used in LIME for spacing
  individual lines. HWN.




@item
Dorothea Blostein and Lippold Haken.
@strong{The Lime Music Editor: A Diagram Editor Involving Complex
  Translations}.
@emph{Software Practice and Experience}, 24(3):289--306, march 1994.
A description of various conversions, decisions and issues relating to this
  interactive editor HWN.




@item
Nabil Bouzaiene, Loïc@tie{}Le Gall, and Emmanuel Saint@tie{}James.
@strong{Une bibliothèque pour la notation musicale baroque}.
LNCS. In @emph{EP '98}, 1998.
Describes ATYS, an extension to Berlioz, that can mimick handwritten baroque
  style beams.




@item
Donald Byrd.
@strong{A System for Music Printing by Computer}.
@emph{Computers and the Humanities}, 8:161-72, 1974.





@item
Donald Byrd.
@strong{Music Notation by Computer}.
PhD thesis, Indiana University, 1985.
Describes the SMUT (sic) system for automated music printout.




@item
Donald Byrd.
@strong{Music Notation Software and Intelligence}.
@emph{Computer Music Journal}, 18(1):17--20, 1994.
Byrd (author of Nightingale) shows four problematic fragments of notation, and
  rants about notation programs that try to exhibit intelligent behaviour. HWN.




@item
Walter@tie{}B Hewlett and Eleanor Selfridge@tie{}Field.
@strong{Directory of Computer Assisted Research in Musicology}.
.
Annual editions since 1985, many containing surveys of music typesetting
  technology. SP.




@item
Alyssa Lamb.
@strong{The University of Colorado Music Engraving page}.
1996.
Webpages about engraving (designed with finale users in mind) (sic) HWN.




@item
Roger@tie{}B. Dannenberg.
@strong{Music Representation: Issues, Techniques, and Systems}.
@emph{Computer Music Journal}, 17(3), 1993.
This article points to some problems and solutions with music representation.
  HWN.




@item
Michael Droettboom.
@strong{Study of music Notation Description Languages}.
Technical Report, 2000.
GUIDO and lilypond compared. LilyPond wins on practical issues as usability and
  availability of tools, GUIDO wins on implementation simplicity.




@item
R.@tie{}F. Ericson.
@strong{The DARMS Project: A status report}.
@emph{Computing in the humanities}, 9(6):291--298, 1975.
Gourlay [gourlay86] writes: A discussion of the design and potential uses of
  the DARMS music-description language.




@item
H.S. Field@tie{}Richards.
@strong{Cadenza: A Music Description Language}.
@emph{Computer Music Journal}, 17(4), 1993.
A description through examples of a music entry language. Apparently it has no
  formal semantics. There is also no implementation of notation convertor. HWN.




@item
Miguel Filgueiras.
@strong{Some Music Typesetting Algorithms}.
.





@item
Miguel Filgueiras and José@tie{}Paulo Leal.
@strong{Representation and manipulation of music documents in SceX}.
@emph{Electronic Publishing}, 6(4):507--518, 1993.





@item
Miguel Filgueiras.
@strong{Implementing a Symbolic Music Processing System}.
1996.





@item
Eric Foxley.
@strong{Music --- A language for typesetting music scores}.
@emph{Software --- Practice and Experience}, 17(8):485-502, 1987.
A paper on a simple TROFF preprocessor to typeset music.




@item
Loïc@tie{}Le Gall.
@strong{Création d'une police adaptée à la notation musicale baroque}.
Master's thesis, École Estienne, 1997.





@item
Martin Gieseking.
@strong{Code-basierte Generierung interaktiver Notengraphik}.
PhD thesis, Universität Osnabrück, 2001.





@item
David@tie{}A. Gomberg.
@strong{A Computer-Oriented System for Music Printing}.
PhD thesis, Washington University, 1975.





@item
David@tie{}A. Gomberg.
@strong{A Computer-oriented System for Music Printing}.
@emph{Computing and the Humanities}, 11:63-80, march 1977.
Gourlay [gourlay86] writes: "A discussion of the problems of representing the
  conventions of musical notation in computer algorithms.".




@item
John.@tie{}S. Gourlay.
@strong{A language for music printing}.
@emph{Communications of the ACM}, 29(5):388--401, 1986.
This paper describes the MusiCopy musicsetting system and an input language to
  go with it.




@item
John@tie{}S. Gourlay, A. Parrish, D. Roush, F. Sola, and Y. Tien.
@strong{Computer Formatting of Music}.
Technical Report OSU-CISRC-2/87-TR3, Department of Computer and Information
  Science, The Ohio State University, 1987.
This paper discusses the development of algorithms for the formatting of
  musical scores (from abstract). It also appeared at PROTEXT III, Ireland
  1986.




@item
John@tie{}S. Gourlay.
@strong{Spacing a Line of Music,}.
Technical Report OSU-CISRC-10/87-TR35, Department of Computer and Information
  Science, The Ohio State University, 1987.





@item
John Grøver.
@strong{A computer-oriented description of Music Notation. Part III: Accidental
  Positioning}.
Technical Report 135, Department of informatics, University of Oslo, 1989.
Placement of accidentals crystallised in an enormous set of rules. Same remarks
  as for [grover89-twovoices] applies.




@item
John Grøver.
@strong{A computer-oriented description of Music Notation. Part I. The Symbol
  Inventory}.
Technical Report 133, Department of informatics, University of Oslo, 1989.
The goal of this series of reports is a full description of music formatting.
  As these largely depend on parameters of fonts, it starts with a verbose
  description of music symbols. The subject is treated backwards: from general
  rules of typesetting the author tries to extract dimensions for characters,
  whereas the rules of typesetting (in a particular font) follow from the
  dimensions of the symbols. His symbols do not match (the stringent)
  constraints formulated by eg. [wanske].




@item
John Grøver.
@strong{A computer-oriented description of Music Notation. Part II: Two Voice
  Sharing a Staff, Leger Line Rules, Dot Positioning}.
Technical Report 134, Department of informatics, University of Oslo, 1989.
A lot rules for what is in the title are formulated. The descriptions are long
  and verbose. The verbosity shows that formulating specific rules is not the
  proper way to approach the problem. Instead, the formulated rules should
  follow from more general rules, similar to [parrish87-simultaneities].




@item
Lippold Haken and Dorothea Blostein.
@strong{The Tilia Music Representation: Extensibility, Abstraction, and
  Notation Contexts for the Lime Music Editor}.
@emph{Computer Music Journal}, 17(3):43--58, 1993.





@item
Lippold Haken and Dorothea Blostein.
@strong{A New Algorithm for Horizontal Spacing of Printed Music}.
Banff, In @emph{International Computer Music Conference}, pages 118-119, Sept
  1995.
This describes an algorithm which uses springs between adjacent columns.




@item
Wael@tie{}A. Hegazy.
@strong{On the Implementation of the MusiCopy Language Processor,}.
Technical Report OSU-CISRC-10/87-TR34, Department of Computer and Information
  Science, The Ohio State University, 1987.
Describes the "parser" which converts MusiCopy MDL to MusiCopy Simultaneities
  and columns. MDL is short for Music Description Language [gourlay86]. It
  accepts music descriptions that are organised into measures filled with
  voices, which are filled with notes. The measures can be arranged
  simultaneously or sequentially. To address the 2-dimensionality, almost all
  constructs in MDL must be labeled. MDL uses begin/end markers for attribute
  values and spanners. Rightfully the author concludes that MusiCopy must
  administrate a "state" variable containing both properties and current
  spanning symbols. MusiCopy attaches graphic information to the objects
  constructed in the input: the elements of the input are partially complete
  graphic objects.




@item
Wael@tie{}A. Hegazy and John@tie{}S. Gourlay.
@strong{Optimal line breaking in music}.
Technical Report OSU-CISRC-8/87-TR33, Department of Computer and Information
  Science, The Ohio State University,, 1987.





@item
Wael@tie{}A. Hegazy and John@tie{}S. Gourlay. (J.@tie{}C. van Vliet, editor).
@strong{Optimal line breaking in music}.
Cambridge University Press, In @emph{Proceedings of the International
  Conference on Electronic Publishing, Document Manipulation and Typography.
  Nice (France)}, April 1988.





@item
Walter@tie{}B. Hewlett and Eleanor Selfridge@tie{}Field, editors.
@strong{The Virtual Score; representation, retrieval and restoration}.
Computing in Musicology. MIT Press, 2001.





@item
H.@tie{}H. Hoos, K.@tie{}A. Hamel, K. Renz, and J. Kilian.
@strong{The GUIDO Music Notation Format---A Novel Approach for Adequately
  Representing Score-level Music}.
In @emph{Proceedings of International Computer Music Conference}, pages
  451--454, 1998.





@item
Peter@tie{}S. Langston.
@strong{Unix music tools at Bellcore}.
@emph{Software --- Practice and Experience}, 20(S1):47--61, 1990.
This paper deals with some command-line tools for music editing and playback.




@item
Dominique Montel.
@strong{La gravure de la musique, lisibilité esthétique, respect de l'oevre}.
Lyon, In @emph{Musique & Notations}, 1997.





@item
Giovanni Müller.
@strong{Interaktive Bearbeitung konventioneller Musiknotation}.
PhD thesis, Eidgenössische Technische Hochschule Zürich, 1990.
This is about engraver-quality typesetting with computers. It accepts the axiom
  that notation is too difficult to generate automatically. The result is that
  a notation program should be a WYSIWYG editor that allows one to tweak
  everything.




@item
Cindy Grande.
@strong{NIFF6a Notation Interchange File Format}.
Grande Software Inc., 1995.
Specs for NIFF, a reasonably comprehensive but binary format for notation HWN.




@item
Severo@tie{}M. Ornstein and John@tie{}Turner@tie{}Maxwell III.
@strong{Mockingbird: A Composer's Amanuensis}.
Technical Report CSL-83-2, Xerox Palo Alto Research Center, 3333 Coyote Hill
  Road, Palo Alto, CA, 94304, January 1983.





@item
Severo@tie{}M. Ornstein and John@tie{}Turner@tie{}Maxwell III.
@strong{Mockingbird: A Composer's Amanuensis}.
@emph{Byte}, 9, January 1984.
A discussion of an interactive and graphical computer system for music
  composition.




@item
Stephen@tie{}Dowland Page.
@strong{Computer Tools for Music Information Retrieval}.
PhD thesis, Dissertation University of Oxford, 1988.
Don't ask Stephen for a copy. Write to the Bodleian Library, Oxford, or to the
  British Library, instead. SP.




@item
Allen Parish, Wael@tie{}A. Hegazy, John@tie{}S. Gourlay, Dean@tie{}K. Roush,
  and F.@tie{}Javier Sola.
@strong{MusiCopy: An automated Music Formatting System}.
Technical Report, 1987.





@item
A. Parrish and John@tie{}S. Gourlay.
@strong{Computer Formatting of Musical Simultaneities,}.
Technical Report OSU-CISRC-10/87-TR28, Department of Computer and Information
  Science, The Ohio State University, 1987.
This note discusses placement of balls, stems, dots which occur at the same
  moment ("Simultaneity").




@item
Steven Powell.
@strong{Music engraving today}.
Brichtmark, 2002.
A "How Steven uses Finale" manual.




@item
Gary@tie{}M. Rader.
@strong{Creating Printed Music Automatically}.
@emph{Computer}, 29(6):61--69, June 1996.
Describes a system called MusicEase, and explains that it uses "constraints"
  (which go unexplained) to automatically position various elements.




@item
Kai Renz.
@strong{Algorithms and data structures for a music notation system based on
  GUIDO music notation}.
PhD thesis, Universität Darmstadt, 2002.





@item
René Roelofs.
@strong{Een Geautomatiseerd Systeem voor het Afdrukken van Muziek}.
Number 45327. Master's thesis, Erasmus Universiteit Rotterdam, 1991.
This dutch thesis describes a monophonic typesetting system, and focuses on the
  breaking algorithm, which is taken from Hegazy & Gourlay.




@item
Joseph Rothstein.
@strong{Review of Passport Designs' Encore Music Notation Software}.
@emph{Computer Music Journal}, ?.





@item
Dean@tie{}K. Roush.
@strong{Using MusiCopy}.
Technical Report OSU-CISRC-18/87-TR31, Department of Computer and Information
  Science, The Ohio State University, 1987.
User manual of MusiCopy.




@item
D. Roush.
@strong{Music Formatting Guidelines}.
Technical Report OSU-CISRC-3/88-TR10, Department of Computer and Information
  Science, The Ohio State University, 1988.
Rules on formatting music formulated for use in computers. Mainly distilled
  from [Ross] HWN.




@item
Eleanor Selfridge@tie{}Field, editor.
@strong{Beyond MIDI: the handbook of musical codes}.
MIT Press, 1997.
A description of various music interchange formats.




@item
Donald Sloan.
@strong{Aspects of Music Representation in HyTime/SMDL}.
@emph{Computer Music Journal}, 17(4), 1993.
An introduction into HyTime and its score description variant SMDL. With a
  short example that is quite lengthy in SMDL.




@item
International@tie{}Organization for Standardization@tie{}(ISO).
@strong{Information Technology - Document Description and Processing Languages
  - Standard Music Description Language (SMDL)}.
Number ISO/IEC DIS 10743. 1992.





@item
Leland Smith.
@strong{Editing and Printing Music by Computer}, volume 17.
1973.
Gourlay [gourlay86] writes: A discussion of Smith's music-printing system
  SCORE.




@item
F. Sola.
@strong{Computer Design of Musical Slurs, Ties and Phrase Marks,}.
Technical Report OSU-CISRC-10/87-TR32, Department of Computer and Information
  Science, The Ohio State University, 1987.
Overview of a procedure for generating slurs.




@item
F. Sola and D. Roush.
@strong{Design of Musical Beams,}.
Technical Report OSU-CISRC-10/87-TR30, Department of Computer and Information
  Science, The Ohio State University, 1987.
Calculating beam slopes HWN.




@item
Howard Wright.
@strong{how to read and write tab: a guide to tab notation}.
.
FAQ (with answers) about TAB, the ASCII variant of Tablature. HWN.




@item
Geraint Wiggins, Eduardo Miranda, Alaaaan Smaill, and Mitch Harris.
@strong{A Framework for the evaluation of music representation systems}.
@emph{Computer Music Journal}, 17(3), 1993.
A categorisation of music representation systems (languages, OO systems etc)
  split into high level and low level expressiveness. The discussion of Charm
  and parallel processing for music representation is rather vague. HWN.




@end itemize
